            <div xml:id="d193">

                <pb facs="0364" n="264" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg264"/>

                <head>No. 193.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Schuyler to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-06-06"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 6, 1884.</date>
                        (Received June 24.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 77.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my dispatches No. 65, dated
                    February 23, and No. 68, dated March 10, 1884, about the importation of American
                    pork into Greece, I have the honor to inclose herewith a copy and a translation
                    of a note received from the minister of foreign affairs, dated May 19 (31).</p>
                <p>It will be seen by this note that the importation of American pork is permitted
                    only on a certificate of a Greek consul that no disease exists in the place of
                    origin. This rule is applicable to all importations of pork from every country,
                    and, as it existed at the time when the restriction on American pork was
                    abolished, I had already taken it for granted that it applied also to
                    importations from the United States.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>EUGENE SCHUYLER.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d193-a1.xml" xpointer="d193-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
